---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy Policy · b-oss website"
  description="Privacy policy for the b-oss website (b-oss.dev): what it collects, the contact form, and the Blipfoto status page."
  narrow
>
  <Hero label="Privacy hero" title="Privacy Policy — the b-oss website">This policy covers this website, b-oss.dev, including the contact form and the unofficial
      Blipfoto status page. The apps have their own policies, linked below.</Hero>

  <section
    class="block"
    style="border-top: none; padding-top: 0"
    data-screen-label="Privacy body"
  >
    <p><em>Last updated: 29 October 2026</em></p>

    <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
      This website is part of b-oss, an open-source project led by Ian Stevenson. It is
      independent and is not affiliated with or endorsed by Blipfoto. Questions about this
      policy can be sent to
      <a href="mailto:info@b-oss.dev">info@b-oss.dev</a> or through the
      <a href="contact.html">contact page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What the website itself collects</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing of its own. The pages use no analytics, no advertising, no cookies and no tracking,
      and store nothing in your browser. The contact form and the status page involve other
      services, described below.
    </p>

    <h2>Hosting</h2>
    <p>
      The site is hosted on Cloudflare. Like any host, Cloudflare handles technical data about
      each request, such as your IP address, the page you asked for and the time, and may keep
      it in its own logs under
      <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare's privacy policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>The contact form</h2>
    <p>
      If you use the <a href="contact.html">contact form</a>, your browser sends your name,
      email address and message straight to
      <a href="https://web3forms.com/privacy">Web3Forms</a>, which delivers them to us by email.
      We use them only to read and reply to your message. Web3Forms handles the data under its
      own policy. If you want a message deleted, ask through the contact page.
    </p>

    <h2>The status page</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="status.html">status page</a> shows data from a small service the project
      runs on Cloudflare. When you open the page, your browser asks that service for the latest
      figures. Cloudflare records requests to the service in logs that the project can view.
      They can include your IP address, the page asked for and the time. We use those logs only
      to run and troubleshoot the service. Cloudflare keeps them for up to 7 days and handles
      them under <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">its privacy policy</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The page also loads Cloudflare's Turnstile anti-bot check, so that the Share a problem
      form can tell people from bots. Cloudflare runs Turnstile under
      <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">its privacy policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>If you share a problem</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        We store the time, which part had the problem (the Blipfoto website or its API), whether
        it was offline or not working right, and any tags you tick (login, uploads, slow,
        unreliable). There is no free text, and no name, account or email.
      </li>
      <li>
        Those details are kept for a few days. After that only daily totals are kept.
      </li>
      <li>
        We use your IP address briefly to limit repeat reports. It is turned into a salted
        one-way hash, and the service's own database keeps only that hash, not the address (the address can still
        appear in the Cloudflare logs above). The hash is kept for about
        half an hour and deleted soon after, so at most about 45 minutes in total. It is still
        personal data while we hold it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Your IP address is also passed to Cloudflare to check the anti-bot result. Shared
        problems are not sent to Blipfoto.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The monitor's own checks of Blipfoto only read public pages and involve no visitor data.
    </p>

    <h2>The apps</h2>
    <p>The apps have their own policies:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="b-mobile-privacy.html">b-mobile for Blipfoto</a> (Android)</li>
      <li><a href="b-ark-chrome-privacy.html">b-ark-chrome</a> (Chrome extension)</li>
        </ul>

    <h2>b-ark (Windows desktop app)</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        It connects to Blipfoto (its API, your pictures, and the Blipfoto website where a
        backup needs it) to back up your journal. That traffic is between your computer and
        Blipfoto, under Blipfoto's own policy.
      </li>
      <li>
        On your computer it keeps your backups in the folder you choose, its settings, and your
        Blipfoto sign-in details, which are encrypted using the operating system's secure
        storage.
      </li>
      <li>
        It has no analytics or crash reporting and sends nothing to the b-oss project. It
        writes a log file on your computer only.
      </li>
      <li>
        By default it checks for updates by contacting GitHub, where the b-oss releases are
        published, and downloads a new version if there is one. GitHub can see that request
        under
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">its privacy statement</a>.
        You can turn this off in the app's settings.
      </li>
      <li>
        Only after a backup has failed to reach Blipfoto, it checks whether your computer is
        online at all, by sending one standard DNS lookup request (for cloudflare.com) to
        Cloudflare's public resolver at 1.1.1.1. It contains none of your data or sign-in
        details, but Cloudflare can see your IP address under
        <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">its privacy policy</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      For anything about privacy on this site, use the <a href="contact.html">contact page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, the new version will be published at this address with a revised
      date at the top.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
